==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|retret}}, from {{der|en|fro|retrait}} or {{m|fro|retret}}, from {{der|en|la|retractus}}, from {{m|la|retraho}}. {{doublet|en|retract|retrait|ritratto}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈtɹiːt/|/ɹəˈtɹiːt/|/ɹiˈtɹiːt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-retreat.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːt|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[pull back|pulling back]] or [[withdraw]]ing, as from something dangerous, or unpleasant.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|IV|iii|passage=In a '''retreat''' he outruns any lackey.}}
## The act of [[reverse|revers]]ing direction and [[recede|reced]]ing from a forward position.
## {{lb|en|military}} Withdrawal by a military force from a dangerous position or from enemy attack.
##: {{ux|en|The general opted for a swift '''retreat''' because he saw his troops were vastly outnumbered.}}
# A peaceful, quiet place affording privacy or security.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1764|author=Horace Walpole|title=The Castle of Otranto
|passage="{{...}} But come, Lady, we are too near the mouth of the cavern; let us seek its inmost recesses. {{...}}" "Though all your actions are noble, {{...}} is it fitting that I should accompany you alone into these perplexed '''retreats'''? Should we be found together, what would a censorious world think of my conduct?"}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|volume=I|chapter=IV|pages=40–41|pageref=41|passage=In a large bedroom upstairs, the window of which was thickly curtained with a great woollen shawl lately discarded by the landlady, Mrs. Rolliver, were gathered on this evening nearly a dozen persons, all seeking vinous bliss; all old inhabitants of the nearer end of Marlott, and frequenters of this '''retreat'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1692|author=w:Roger L'Estrange|chapter=Fable 100: An Old Man and a Lion|title=Fables of Aesop|page=115
|passage=... he built his son a house of pleasure, on purpose to keep him out of harm's way; and spared neither art nor cost to make it a delicious '''retreat'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|The Flower and the Leaf
|passage=That pleasing shade they sought, a soft '''retreat''' / From sudden April showers, a shelter from the heat.}}
# {{label|en|rare|_|and|_|obsolete|euphemism}} A [[peaceful]], [[quiet]] [[place]] in which to [[urinate]] and [[defecate]]: an [[outhouse]]; a [[lavatory]].
# A period of retirement, seclusion, or solitude.
#: {{ux|en|We both need a week '''retreat''' after those two stressful years working in the city.}}
# A period of meditation, prayer or study.
#* {{quote-web|en|text=For example, Steve Jobs used to run an annual '''retreat''' for what he considered the 100 most important people at Apple, and these were not the 100 people highest on the org chart.|title=Founder Mode|work=Essays|format=blog|date=September 2024|first=Paul|last=Graham|authorlink=Paul Graham (programmer)|url=https://paulgraham.com/foundermode.html}}
# {{lb|en|military}} A signal for a military withdrawal.
# {{lb|en|military}} A bugle call or drumbeat signaling the lowering of the flag at sunset, as on a military base.
#* {{RQ:Wiggin Penelope's Progress|chapter=8|passage=to-morrow the Royal Standard will be hoisted at Edinburgh Castle from reveille to '''retreat'''. }}
# {{lb|en|military}} A military ceremony to lower the flag.
# {{lb|en|chess}} The move of a piece from a threatened position.

